Hiring Team \
Prometheum

Dear Hiring Team,

I am applying for the Software Engineer 1 (Full-Stack) position at Prometheum. I completed a B.S. in Computer Science at Portland State University in Winter 2025, and what I would bring to this role comes from building one application end to end rather than from a prior engineering title. That is precisely why the path this posting describes — starting on small, clearly defined pieces of the TypeScript and React codebase and widening as the system becomes familiar — is the one I am looking for.

The requirement I want to address directly is the judgment to recognize when AI output is wrong, or unnecessary. My most recent project is a full-stack application that converts spoken bar inventory counts into structured records in a relational database. Most transcribed lines in that system are routine matches against a known inventory sheet and do not require a model at all, so I designed a command-driven parsing workflow to resolve them against the sheet directly and reserved the AI for genuinely ambiguous input. That decision reduced AI API calls by roughly 80 percent. I treat generated code on the same terms: useful as a first draft, and not trusted until I have read it and can explain why it does what it does. The application is written in TypeScript, React, and Python, and versioned in Git.

My coursework in systems programming, data structures, and SQL databases is what allows me to reason about a request as it moves from the front end through an API into a database, rather than only recognizing the shape of familiar code. I have no finance or blockchain background and will not claim otherwise. What I have done is work that had to withstand outside review: at Future Generations Collaborative I contributed to federal grant reporting, where the records were read by people whose programs depended on them being accurate. That is a smaller thing than securities regulation, but the instinct it built is the relevant one — in work that is accountable to someone else, the details are the job.

I would be glad to discuss the project in further detail, including the parts I would build differently now.

Sincerely, \
Matt Loera
